Yeast cells are unicellular eukaryotes. Yeast cells respond to changes in the concentrations of the sugars galactose and glucose by switching on genes using transcription factors.

Fig. 5.1 shows the events that occur when the sugar galactose is present and glucose is absent in the external environment.

{{figure:figure-5-1-source-rebuild.png}}

parts.text:
Protein **B** has a similar role to the protein coded for by gene Y of the *lac* operon.

Suggest a possible role for protein **B** that would allow yeast cells to make use of galactose.

{{figure:reviewed_response_2.png}}

Calculate the percentage of Msn2 molecules in the cell that are located inside the nucleus, when glucose is **absent**.

Show your working and write your answer to **two** significant figures.

{{figure:reviewed_response_3.png}}

When glucose is **present**, 26% of Msn2 molecules in the cell are located inside the nucleus.

Suggest why this figure is different from your answer to **(i)**.

{{figure:reviewed_response_4.png}}

Another method of tagging molecules with a fluorescent colour is to use monoclonal antibodies that have GFP attached. These monoclonal antibodies bind specifically to the molecule of interest. This is called immunofluorescent tagging.

Suggest reasons why immunofluorescent tagging is **not** a suitable choice for this investigation.

{{figure:reviewed_response_5.png}}
